#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199241} :activity/id #uuid "74f9ba1c-b4de-59c9-abfb-e43102be1d8d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999 :topic/id #uuid "d21fb3a0-a806-5c0d-82f7-6eced020fb4f" :topic/math-academy-id 4139} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199448 :tutorial/content "Differentiating vector-valued functions is easy: we just differentiate each component separately. So, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\, y(t) \\right>,\n$$\n\n then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf{r} }{\\textrm dt}  = \\mathbf{r}'(t) = \\left< x'(t), \\, y'(t) \\right>.\n$$\n\nFor example, if $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< t+1, \\, t^2+2 \\right>,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the derivative of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point $t$ gives a vector that's tangent to the curve traced out by $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $t.$\n\n![](images/6d/6d7f1a972197ff5a92bb64bd0bab9e6dd779e800be48232124f8fda39fa3c949.png)\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a3dd559-f60a-5a18-8d82-3dc93d7d7487" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1091 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4745692d-4755-5843-b652-badc497f62c4" :step/math-academy-id 2149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199242}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198355 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198230 :question/id #uuid "c737d317-8e09-5ccb-b816-b5f56e925ba9" :question/math-academy-id "e-609"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501223}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "2d430221-0498-5e8c-8fa0-61b7f22e0100" :step/math-academy-id 2151 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199441}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198356 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198231 :question/id #uuid "cde8f304-4937-5eb7-a8e1-a08355ee4d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-611"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0e2596-73d6-540c-af84-b19a943da6a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501242}] :knowledge-point/title "The Second Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "10220786-cf3d-5712-b398-62d47674f692" :step/math-academy-id 2153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199449 :tutorial/content "We can differentiate vector-valued functions with three components in the same way. \n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\big\\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: z(t) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nis our vector-valued function, then the derivative is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\text{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\text{d}t} = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{ \\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}, \\: \\dfrac{ \\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\: \\dfrac{ \\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, suppose that we are given the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left\\langle t^3, \\:  \\ln(t+3), \\:  \\sqrt{t}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo compute the derivative $\\mathbf f'\\!(t),$ we find the derivative of each component function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{d}{dt}⟨{t}^{3},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3),\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3)),\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f81076bc-29eb-53de-950f-4dd6e7b16816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2110 :tutorial/title "Derivatives of Vector-Valued Functions"} :step/id #uuid "5f0730aa-e9f5-5a08-bce6-78e83d82631e" :step/math-academy-id 8190 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198473 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198227 :question/id #uuid "a5637d71-22d4-548e-ad36-d09bff61d643" :question/math-academy-id "e-2398"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b31f2bdc-c267-5fc7-bd0f-283777ee4fee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501190}] :knowledge-point/title "The Derivative of a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "5d1be177-bebe-5aaf-9bfe-1f0622f69332" :step/math-academy-id 8191 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199243}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198483}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139353} :activity/id #uuid "753f3c2c-963f-5bf9-82db-0290639a9a4b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049281 :topic/id #uuid "e9994744-cb83-57d9-8154-ea5ac4c05cc8" :topic/math-academy-id 2274} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139717 :tutorial/content "A good example of a direct variation model is the distance an object travels when moving at a constant rate. In this case, the corresponding direct variation equation is \n\n$$\nd=vt,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $d$ is the total distance traveled by the object,\n\n- $t$ is the total time that the object has been traveling, and,\n\n- $v$ is the constant of proportionality, called the **speed** of the object.\n\nFor instance, if an object travels a distance of $24\\,\\textrm m$ in $4$ seconds, then we can substitute $d=24\\,\\textrm m$ and $t=4\\,\\textrm s$ into the above equation and solve for $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =v\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{24}{4} & =v \\\\ 6 & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation that relates $d$ and $t$ is\n\n$$\nd=6t.\n$$\n\n \n\nOne thing to note is that the constant of proportionality $v = 6$ also has units. So let's work out what these units are.\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\nd = vt,\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{d}{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, the units of $v$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm m}{\\textrm s} = \\textrm m / \\textrm s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality (including its unit) is $v = 6\\, \\textrm m / \\textrm s.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcda3158-97b6-5a4a-b062-3803a3db7b26" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8753 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "577c6468-9f0e-55fb-84fc-17fcc4e6bca8" :step/math-academy-id 196782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138430 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138166 :question/id #uuid "f01f91de-9010-5429-ba83-faaf90c866d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14086"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049141}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499543}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"} :step/id #uuid "fd7ad040-afed-5ba6-ad72-f3ed0909fc54" :step/math-academy-id 196783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138431 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138168 :question/id #uuid "f1408bad-ae5c-55fb-b78e-60ee0d8a1ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14088"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7df32abc-07bd-5213-a838-50e893ff5759" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499547}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Direct Variation"} :step/id #uuid "d2efc257-0a27-570d-a356-98d12f63e6c2" :step/math-academy-id 196786 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139719 :tutorial/content "When modeling proportional relationships, we sometimes find that a quantity $y$ varies directly with the *square* of a second quantity $x.$ In such cases, we write\n\n$$\ny = kx^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a circle of radius $r,$ measured in centimeters.\n\n![](images/fa/fad3151a754d96d3e4ad97fa812ce7087601cebe7c4ab00f7b3c8cd603b140c0.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the area $A,$ measured in square meters, of a circle varies directly with the *square* of the radius $r.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\nA = k r^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5a8d87f-edfb-5e37-ad6d-6cafde48e4ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8755 :tutorial/title "Variations With Second Powers"} :step/id #uuid "80f09102-b1f8-5a4e-8df1-f285f0944581" :step/math-academy-id 196789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138432 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138171 :question/id #uuid "201cba9a-1d9e-5bf3-b312-d84fb3d59407" :question/math-academy-id "e-14091"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01db3ebe-66ec-5340-8e29-c99fb149f5da" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499532}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Direct Variations With Second Powers"} :step/id #uuid "458a0b34-716e-5233-8ee6-52e6a9afd6a3" :step/math-academy-id 196790}] :activity/title "Modeling with Direct Variation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138600}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079512} :activity/id #uuid "580243c0-444d-51d9-820f-ab39d2e434fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375 :topic/id #uuid "eff38647-ee17-5513-ad67-94525b17bd40" :topic/math-academy-id 621} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079690 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that $f(2)=5$ and $f'(2)=1.$ Based on this information, how can we approximate the value of $f(2.75)?$\n\n![](images/25/25baabc5d4193a480d09370faa104d34edc14b2aa09759f5976d592a387fb398.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\ny-f(a) = f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the above, we get \n\n$$\ny= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $x$ is close to $a,$ we can assume that $y\\approx f(x) ,$ which gives us the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe often represent the tangent line approximation using the notation $L(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we want the tangent line approximation at $a=2.$ Substituting this value along with the given information, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =(5)+(1)(x-2) \\\\  & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the graphs. Notice that in the neighborhood of $x=2$, the function and its tangent line approximation are very close. The line $L(x)$ is a good approximation of the function for points close to $x=2.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1de31c316c019e8faf466f64a0e6ff24a29935a7ec354c2acdf18d3069b8d47.png)\n\nAs we can see, ${\\color{blue}L(2.75)}$ and ${\\color{red}f(2.75)}$ are quite close. So, we can approximate $f(2.75)$ using $L(2.75)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.75) & ≈L(2.75) \\\\  & =(2.75)+3 \\\\  & =5.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nis sometimes also called the **linear approximation** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a.$ The process of approximating the function by its tangent is called **linearization.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fd16cbf-e232-56c9-84ba-d6b6ff2d147a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 579 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "80d363a5-ade6-554f-b554-78362a2a4a87" :step/math-academy-id 4825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :question/id #uuid "aea27069-47b4-50b0-9e70-3a65951916b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534005}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "29daedd8-9104-56cf-8960-bc6517ab9536" :step/math-academy-id 4826 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078567 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :question/id #uuid "44510fee-857c-5182-8f9e-a08c18966142" :question/math-academy-id "e-292"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492912}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "0f65d689-6eed-51f9-99e7-de9d4bff5796" :step/math-academy-id 4829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079691 :tutorial/content "Let $L(x)$ be the tangent line approximation of $y = f(x)$ at $x=a,$ as shown in the pictures below. \n\n| ![](images/ad/ad7375c544eed881d96d07b449d9003c7df813bdddcdaf29e82bfda6bd4cb283.png) | ![](images/b5/b5b3cd71da3243a5848021831403adde07f1487ff1077f3359d2564ba0f4b686.png) |\n| --- | --- |\n\nWe can make the following conclusions:\n\n- When the function is *concave up*, the tangent line is *below* the graph (see the picture on the left). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\lt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *underestimate* of the function.\n\n- When the function is *concave down*, the tangent line is *above* the graph (see the picture on the right). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\gt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *overestimate* of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "717ddca8-0e12-5d53-9384-635de0a676ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 582 :tutorial/title "Underestimate and Overestimate Approximations"} :step/id #uuid "539a8b72-5dd7-5bd3-b6ff-ecd4fb4535ed" :step/math-academy-id 4832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078236 :question/id #uuid "dc311e80-fd79-5b89-9f0e-0b1caef6457e" :question/math-academy-id "e-293"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049011}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492906}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"} :step/id #uuid "0a4e1771-2f14-58d7-9a7d-62eb186154dd" :step/math-academy-id 4833}] :activity/title "Approximating Functions Using Local Linearity and Linearization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078647}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492978} :activity/id #uuid "455ca42c-7c76-5300-91b9-ae6aa04f859f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375 :topic/id #uuid "eff38647-ee17-5513-ad67-94525b17bd40" :topic/math-academy-id 621} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b421bb4d-d57d-58c5-91d5-941ee935054f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492577} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a49ba44-a18c-572f-9889-3ebe1220fd5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d382317e-adb1-5580-86d4-be3a012ea508" :question/math-academy-id "q-346053" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ and $f(2)=5.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ to estimate the value of $f(2.4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(2.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=5,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)=3(2)^{2}-2(2)=12-4=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=5+8(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =5+8(2.4-2) \\\\  & =5+8(0.4) \\\\  & =5+3.2 \\\\  & =8.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0d9ad38c-433d-50ba-89b8-79d1f4ef9ffa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be0819e-e32e-5ef6-97d4-15b2e23dacdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492269} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecca4ab-9aea-5781-96bb-820669fd7e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492436} {:answer/id #uuid "8500dd81-e29c-5504-be31-e4e0a9a0eb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492484} {:answer/id #uuid "95cb96a6-c568-5bb0-952f-972cc891d363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492505} {:answer/id #uuid "ceca9693-3483-5422-9145-bd8fbfe73fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492436} :answer-field/id #uuid "59fa50a0-4d01-5c7c-859f-4dbcf823f9f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492738} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14f2a850-012a-5580-b8a7-57dc089f382e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492256} {:answer/id #uuid "999c6a53-0e3f-57e7-ab55-c9f41de35cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492256} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a25890-8790-51f7-a94b-8b42c7efdcea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bfc00702-2e19-5641-9f78-a3bfcfb36d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346717" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,1)$ and is concave up on the interval $(-1,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=1,$ estimate the value of $f(-0.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n\n$\\,f(-0.3)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,1),$ so we have $f(0)=1.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =1+1(x-0) \\\\  & =1+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(-0.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-0.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.3) & ≈L(-0.3) \\\\  & =1+(-0.3) \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(-1,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{underestimate}.$"} :step/id #uuid "6b42d425-4cde-5eb5-be9e-2e8616d2acbb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a547162-cfa3-59b2-bf37-5f807c2a4ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is concave down in the neighborhood of $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492303} {:answer/id #uuid "3920e40c-6417-5dae-af53-10980d572249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492339} {:answer/id #uuid "64cdcc2c-abc6-529e-a57a-d38b505a0e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492418} {:answer/id #uuid "7478861d-f3b8-551f-87de-db08ec6d1ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is concave up in the neighborhood of $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492450} {:answer/id #uuid "75b59e00-4955-5185-9a45-7fe9f929117b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is increasing in the neighborhood of $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492450} :answer-field/id #uuid "a316d6e9-3769-5f80-845f-4a7c1ae14f3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2fd9627c-83fd-577e-93d9-1dea3b352d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48045" :question/problem "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=3$ underestimates the function in the neighborhood of $x=3,$ which of the following must be true?" :question/worked-solution "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ underestimates the values of this function in the neighborhood of $x=3$, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)<f(x),\n$$\n\nfor all $x$ close to (but not equal to) $x=3.$ In other words, the line $L(x)$ must be *below* the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave up in the neighborhood of $x=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "8fe411e2-028a-531d-ad89-3fff08a9c9f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45fe17a1-d11e-5ef2-a485-5fbcd2100bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364228} {:answer/id #uuid "d516aced-a928-5914-9082-f564a782a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364229} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ba16b8-0f05-5dd7-b702-a0bd9e5f4c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364230} {:answer/id #uuid "34896b00-823a-5083-8e9e-bc2fc0d9d7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364231} {:answer/id #uuid "effc20e5-6c6d-58bc-9b08-56dab08a74ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364230} :answer-field/id #uuid "496c5022-25df-547f-b02e-e179117a549f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c05a076-435e-50b5-bc51-d3ae092e8ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-4814" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-6$ and $f(1)=-2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(2).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=-2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-6,$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=12(1)^{2}-6=6.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-2+6(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ≈L(2) \\\\  & =-2+6(2-1) \\\\  & =-2+6(1) \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a5424259-87b3-5634-8eb1-23ea6d96d73b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0f6466d-f440-58c1-8723-cde1e85101c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399880} {:answer/id #uuid "1906a013-62f9-568e-95ae-98c68b274de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399881} {:answer/id #uuid "261b2021-339d-51c1-a9b8-1c77073b9824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399882} {:answer/id #uuid "d631bbed-d22a-5f68-8054-c6e4993d2296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399883} {:answer/id #uuid "87b365d2-efb1-55b5-ba28-a027ed309d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399882} :answer-field/id #uuid "4547a518-52cd-55fa-a5a7-64752ebe189c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a7b7e632-32b9-5dd9-98c7-3b49b8d1e749" :question/math-academy-id "q-115348" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ estimate the value of $f(\\sqrt{2})$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(0,5),$ so we have $f(0)=5.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =5+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x-0) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(\\sqrt{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{2}) & ≈L(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =5+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an overestimate."} :step/id #uuid "a66bdb1b-7763-515f-9f1c-bbad66d3c6ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492932}}] :activity/title "Approximating Functions Using Local Linearity and Linearization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492208}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176696} :activity/id #uuid "83dd2dd3-f6da-548c-a5b5-6bcce212b8d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049954 :topic/id #uuid "211d8970-d274-5653-85b6-8092cf9271f9" :topic/math-academy-id 3581} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175791 :question/id #uuid "95ddf955-ea59-5ac8-977d-767e46b92bf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6159"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050617}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403620}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"} :step/id #uuid "8974bd62-9000-5d83-b9b9-a59c1dd304cc" :step/math-academy-id 22993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176982 :tutorial/content "When a particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O,$ it moves in one of two directions:\n\n- the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x$ (positive direction) when its velocity is positive, and\n\n- the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x$ (negative direction) when its velocity is negative.\n\nSuppose the position of the particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\ns(t) = 3t^2-t^3, \\qquad t > 0.\n$$\n\nRecall that we find the velocity of the particle by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =6t-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the resulting velocity function above, we can find some characteristics of the particle.\n\n- *Time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$*\n\nThis occurs when its velocity is positive. Solving the inequality $v(t) > 0$ for the particle described above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ 6t-3{t}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 3t(2-t) & >0 \\\\ 2-t & >0 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote we can divide through by $t$ without changing the inequality since $t > 0.$ Therefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- *Time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$*\n\nThis occurs when its velocity is negative. Solving the inequality $v(t) < 0$ for the particle described above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 6t-3{t}^{2} & <0 \\\\ 3t(2-t) & <0 \\\\ 2-t & <0 \\\\ t & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote we can divide through by $t$ without changing the inequality since $t > 0.$ Therefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (2,\\infty).$\n\n- *Moments when the particle is stationary.*\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =3{t}^{2}-6t \\\\ 0 & =3t(t-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=0$ and $t=2.$ So the particle is stationary at $t=0\\,\\textrm{s}$ and $t=2\\,\\textrm{s}.$\n\n- *Moments when the acceleration of the particle is zero.*\n\nFor the particle above, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we put $a=0$ into the above to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6-6t \\\\ 6t & =6 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "149d19bc-a7d1-54c8-a008-ab950cb7862d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5201 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "67864534-d946-52bb-aba8-6135e342d069" :step/math-academy-id 32678 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176096 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175755 :question/id #uuid "7a8775a6-f16c-5fc8-865b-89728d73a2c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-213"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050901}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341920}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"} :step/id #uuid "5a2ee73c-16fe-5817-b8e4-5e5fc4cc8970" :step/math-academy-id 869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175756 :question/id #uuid "a6a4547d-a81a-5c9a-9ab1-2bb20d847fca" :question/math-academy-id "e-217"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403502}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "df8bd4cb-a4be-5293-8a8c-7663297896a1" :step/math-academy-id 884}] :activity/title "Determining Characteristics of Moving Objects Using Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176128}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065039} :activity/id #uuid "85ccb72f-560b-5fa9-9d56-a28872650bd7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057 :topic/id #uuid "29efff6a-8254-59b2-b3ad-421a4dead37e" :topic/math-academy-id 283} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065411 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x) = x^2.$ The **definite integral** **of** $\\mathbf f(x)$ **between** $\\mathbf{x=1}$ and $\\mathbf{x=2}$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this definite integral can be interpreted as the **signed area** bounded between the curve $y=f(x),$ the lines $x=1$ and $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fdff7865deed3d5329495bac5dfe045fefca9c09c6fccb7cd02b73d40de8bf69.png)\n\nThe area is taken as positive (with a plus sign) if the corresponding region lies above the $x$-axis and as negative (with a minus sign) if the region lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTo calculate this area, we use a theorem known as the **fundamental theorem of calculus**, which states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on an interval $[a,b]$, and there exists a function $F(x)$ such that $F'(x) = f(x)$ on $[a,b]$, then*\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = F(b) - F(a).\n$$\n\nSo how do we use this to calculate our definite integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x?$ We just need to follow a few steps:\n\n** Step 1**: Find the *indefinite* integral (also known as the antiderivative) of the function using the power rule. We can ignore the constant of integration.\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Write the lower and upper limit next to the result.\n\n$$\n\\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}\\Bigg|_{\\color{red}1}^{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Evaluate the antiderivative between the upper and lower limit, and subtract them.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^2 f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac73.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f56a418c-ec4e-53fe-a09f-f086cc850717" :tutorial/math-academy-id 766 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22b51993-3c46-5284-9c48-3489dd99ada3" :step/math-academy-id 1469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064196 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064068 :question/id #uuid "e2c444fd-31b5-505f-844d-313f9efb7f45" :question/math-academy-id "e-409"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67bbd172-1940-5a1e-82f8-ffc73058f009" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536821}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Polynomial Function"} :step/id #uuid "00e56579-4d9f-5de4-b9aa-a17606a7eebc" :step/math-academy-id 1470 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064197 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064069 :question/id #uuid "e0bb9783-9d30-5139-a2c0-aae55ea18733" :question/math-academy-id "e-410"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bd1f67-6361-55f7-ba25-07e54aad8f66" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332680}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "3e88c067-b3b5-52fb-80d2-5c2b4d44ca37" :step/math-academy-id 1473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064070 :question/id #uuid "77d37915-81e0-5415-8377-a3aae532e1f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1113b2b6-ac10-5599-9e94-5bbed95e32a5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317507}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Radical Function"} :step/id #uuid "86a71506-cd25-5eb2-a0a9-29c4c0c00750" :step/math-academy-id 1475}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064390}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084153} :activity/id #uuid "ddf8dd4c-877a-598f-8893-a8ab19b3b308" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162 :topic/id #uuid "3308e65f-61fe-550b-8dc0-4e4a4925cb49" :topic/math-academy-id 799} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084391 :tutorial/content "Remember that to find the slope $m$ of a tangent line to a function $y=f(x),$ we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = f'(x)\n$$\n\nLikewise, to find the slope $m_n$ of a normal line to a function $y=f(x),$ we take the negative reciprocal of the tangent slope:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(x)}\n$$\n\nWith parametric equations $x=x(t), \\, y=y(t),$ it works the same way. The slope of the tangent line is still the derivative of $y$ with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nLikewise, the slope of the normal line is still given by the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{x'(t)}{y'(t)}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "48431a98-af5d-5fb6-a9c9-0c0a82d65d84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1071 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c94cfe31-d0e9-529d-b6dd-34491bfb6e2d" :step/math-academy-id 623442 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083056 :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527993}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "891625df-94b1-5558-ba83-17e23764feaa" :step/math-academy-id 623443 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084207}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083057 :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528013}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "175eff7b-218c-5515-9477-5ac8c765204a" :step/math-academy-id 623450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083074 :question/id #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" :question/math-academy-id "e-6240"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528000}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"} :step/id #uuid "69ed9071-e55a-5c8e-b0b2-bfbf686b3484" :step/math-academy-id 623447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075 :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523091} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527979}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"} :step/id #uuid "fadade9f-f512-5c24-92c8-f8a0cc3f8c5f" :step/math-academy-id 623454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084467 :tutorial/content "The slope $m$ of the tangent to a parametric curve $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to find all of the locations where the tangent to the curve is horizontal. If the tangent is horizontal, this means that $m=0,$ and so provided that $x'(t)\\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = 0\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad y'(t) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to find the locations where the tangent is horizontal, we solve the equation $y'(t) = 0.$ We also need to double-check that $x'(t)\\neq 0$ for each solution. The final step is necessary because a slope of $\\dfrac00$ is meaningless, and we cannot deduce any information regarding the tangent slope from this expression.\n\nSimilarly, if we want to find the locations where the tangent line is *vertical,* we solve the equation $x'(t)=0,$ and we also need to check that $y'(t)\\neq 0$ for each solution.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc76ffb8-75bd-55f8-8af4-6d84235bd0cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3964 :tutorial/title "Horizontal or Vertical Tangents to Parametric Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a295440a-1435-58d0-bb64-ea293bda1d52" :step/math-academy-id 623446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084201}}] :activity/title "Calculating Tangent and Normal Lines with Parametric Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083473}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069808} :activity/id #uuid "bea02a45-ef63-58e9-9f2c-b150acec23b9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050171 :topic/id #uuid "33d5c327-dce4-5785-8656-637262238322" :topic/math-academy-id 475} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070009 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx(t) = t+1, \\qquad y(t) = t^2+2, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThese parametric equations trace out a curve in the $xy$-plane. We can combine these into a single function, called a **vector-valued** function $\\mathbf r (t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t)\\rangle  =  \\langle t+1, \\: t^2+2\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf r (t)$ takes a **parameter** $t$ as input and returns a **position vector**. For a given value of $t,$ each value of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has its tail at the origin and its terminal point at the coordinates \n$(t+1, \\: t^2+2).$\n\n![](images/10/1023d1a1898a402b95d8374f6242d494f714cde9c5ad4cb8186567b9ea192431.png)\n\nEvaluating vector-valued functions is straightforward. Just remember that the output is a vector, not a scalar. \n\nFor the vectors in the graph above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-3) & =⟨(-3)+1,\\,(-3)^{2}+2⟩=⟨-2,\\,11⟩, \\\\ r(0) & =⟨(0)+1,\\,(0)^{2}+2⟩=⟨1,\\,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨(2)+1,\\,(2)^{2}+2⟩=⟨3,\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5439d77a-b766-5dc3-ad68-6facc632d0f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7721 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f95d8fa-a5ab-52bb-954e-880438fde745" :step/math-academy-id 44768 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :question/id #uuid "6eea01b2-bc2a-55a8-9bab-6789df00fc42" :question/math-academy-id "e-12450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ccd52-63db-5d28-b78b-2df7caf52ad0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427593}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "4e87da40-1bc7-5937-9854-566b24b722d7" :step/math-academy-id 44769 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070010 :tutorial/content "The **magnitude** of a vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t) \\right>,\n$$\n\ndenoted $|\\mathbf{r}(t)|,$ is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of a three-dimensional vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t), \\: z(t) \\right>\n$$\n\nis defined similarly:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2+(z(t))^2}\n$$\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation that's often used for the magnitude of a vector-valued function is $||\\mathbf r(t) ||.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1aaa056f-942b-569d-a4db-4051ddf994ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7722 :tutorial/title "The Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "2f3b3f68-570e-520e-95c8-902f36d55bbc" :step/math-academy-id 44772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :question/id #uuid "86e423e7-db4b-5fdf-a285-bded3bd8161e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e520dc67-3b63-5ca7-959b-f642d85529d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372984}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "2e5ebb70-65ff-51e9-aa1f-59730dada6c1" :step/math-academy-id 44773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068459 :question/id #uuid "ab3899d3-8838-57ad-8a17-b8ac2934c0f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12452"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26571ec1-e645-5f07-ba4e-75c22558394a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050173}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427592}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "063e1808-5b72-584e-a9c3-d3888f153383" :step/math-academy-id 44776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070011 :tutorial/content "We can also define vector-valued functions with three components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = t \\ \\mathbf{i} + 2t^2 \\ \\mathbf{j} + 3t^3 \\ \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nThis particular definition uses the so-called $\\mathbf{i}$-$\\mathbf{j}$-$\\mathbf{k}$ notation.\n\nWe can represent the same function using **angle bracket** notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle t,  \\: 2t^2, \\: 3t^3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAnother notation that's often convenient is **bracket** notation:\n\n![](images/a9/a95fd21b6ea5e7eae150b516d7d0ad82d283a31c895c9223abbeac81b622bb7d.svg)\n\nIt's important to realize that all three notations represent the same function." :tutorial/id #uuid "93d7dd93-747b-51cf-b634-a766d1aef404" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7723 :tutorial/title "Three-Component Functions"} :step/id #uuid "677f9f3d-0e2b-5d85-b640-373f0553cba4" :step/math-academy-id 44780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069813}}] :activity/title "Defining Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068999}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184906} :activity/id #uuid "8038dbd6-9c0f-5fe5-be97-e9a0082ee6ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321 :topic/id #uuid "3faedd4b-6445-5693-abb6-4c7947c1914f" :topic/math-academy-id 3769} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185366 :tutorial/content "How can we calculate the integral of a sum of functions, like $\\displaystyle \\int (x+x^3)\\,\\textrm d x$?\n\nAccording to the **sum rule**, the integral of the sum of functions is equal to the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\int (f(x) \\pm g(x))\\,\\textrm d x = \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm \\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(x + x^{3})dx &= \\intxdx + \\intx^{3}dx \\\\\n&= (\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + c_{1}) + (\\frac{x^{4}}{4} + c_{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{4}x^{4} + (c_{1} + c_{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{4}x^{4} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we renamed\n\n$$\nc_1+c_2=C\n$$\n\n(The sum of two arbitrary constants is itself an arbitrary constant.)" :tutorial/id #uuid "207bbd12-158a-58be-b000-82cace6dcdde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 701 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "61a29b78-51b2-509a-bb12-5c2009c1ee92" :step/math-academy-id 1351 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184057 :question/id #uuid "e4a94ca8-82ee-5776-a46d-b735d4ab60fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-371"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234303}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"} :step/id #uuid "319d1f65-0963-5490-81bd-5f970237cb5a" :step/math-academy-id 1352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185367 :tutorial/content "For more complicated functions consisting of the sum of functions multiplied by constants, we can combine the constant factor and sum rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int k_1 f(x) \\pm k_2 g(x)\\,\\textrm d x = k_1\\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm k_2\\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b5ce4c9-ef44-52d9-a506-96aa15055e00" :tutorial/math-academy-id 703 :tutorial/title "Combining the Constant Factor and Sum Rules"} :step/id #uuid "22856be8-19a5-564b-abcd-7c797b0d4fa8" :step/math-academy-id 1354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184058 :question/id #uuid "ea7dadee-2b44-5e62-bef4-26fb0cb185ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-372"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a58c0a18-4221-50f1-b31c-ae58d099d79d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum and Constant Factor Rules"} :step/id #uuid "9484fd24-4333-5bcd-94ff-4d3a59b12f87" :step/math-academy-id 1355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184059 :question/id #uuid "8f99b603-2fba-5a58-9966-b91d3a8a773e" :question/math-academy-id "e-373"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "007f3355-427a-5da4-8af7-22581f1cb1de" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250124}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sums of Power Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2dbf153e-76a0-59b0-be0c-70a7074b3f96" :step/math-academy-id 1357}] :activity/title "The Sum Rule for Indefinite Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184368}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084147} :activity/id #uuid "4631a63f-ad43-556f-95cf-d62364c255c3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531 :topic/id #uuid "509b8ea0-3cc6-5f94-8f44-a3e6445a83e5" :topic/math-academy-id 798} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084390 :tutorial/content "A curve or function is defined parametrically if $x$ and $y$ are given in terms of a third parameter $t.$ This can often give rise to some interesting shapes. \n\nFor example, we can define a curve as \n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nIf we plot the curve, it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b3/b3d085ad763c368d7fafaca878c364fc13a0c83ca73b2fd17a2f349cb402a16e.png)\n\nIf we're given a parametric curve, how do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}?$ The answer is to use the chain rule. \n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange this formula into a nicer version, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{1}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use the above formula to calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for the given parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2-3, \\qquad y'(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the curve is defined in terms of the parameter $t,$ the derivative is also defined in terms of $t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94a5a3d6-2fcd-522e-934f-f2bf8a8569c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1068 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fbbf0416-db87-510c-8e34-a3647e51dda7" :step/math-academy-id 644784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083054 :question/id #uuid "15c029df-b002-51cc-87db-a94d40a02771" :question/math-academy-id "e-595"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522922} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522920}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "4f8fcd3c-4b1b-56da-86a6-82a4b1a8cdce" :step/math-academy-id 644785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :question/id #uuid "148e5378-9177-59ba-9b1e-3633c940babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433352}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "220ce614-bb7b-5483-bf5e-4f67ec32c8e7" :step/math-academy-id 644788}] :activity/title "Differentiating Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083472}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065296} :activity/id #uuid "a7864d4e-1ca3-5563-b60f-acda20a275e8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675 :topic/id #uuid "5e05a47e-5905-5eee-b6ea-48c720a8d288" :topic/math-academy-id 303} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065376 :tutorial/content "Let's learn how to differentiate the inverse trigonometric functions: $\\arcsin x,$ $\\arccos x,$ and $\\arctan x.$\n\nWe begin with arcsine. From the definition, we know that writing $y=\\arcsin{x}$ is equivalent to writing\n\n$$\n\\sin y=x.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we've found the derivative of $\\arcsin x$ in terms of $y,$ but we want it to be expressed in terms of $x$ only. \n\nTo do that, we use the trigonometric identity $\\sin^2 y+\\cos^2y= 1$ and the fact that $\\sin y =x.$ We write $\\cos y$ as\n\n$$\n\\cos y= \\sqrt{1- \\sin^2 y} = \\sqrt{1 -x^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the derivative of a function at a point equals the slope of the function's graph at that point. Since the graph of $\\arcsin{x}$ has a positive slope everywhere in its domain, we take the positive square root.\n\n![](images/7a/7a974808c3f52c5544048306c044e3079f94caada98ae289116fa2f5fe4f5442.png)\n\nNow, if we plug the last expression into our derivative of $y,$ we get the derivative of $\\arcsin x$ in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same technique to find the derivatives of $\\arccos x$ and $\\arctan x.$ Try it for yourself! The results are given below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}x) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}x) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To remember the derivative formulas above, it's helpful to notice the following:\n\n- The derivative of $\\arccos x$ is just like the derivative of $\\arcsin x,$ except that it's negative. The negative sign makes sense because $\\arccos x$ is a \"co\"-function, and the derivative of a \"co\"-function is negative (for example, the derivative of $\\cos x$ is negative).\n\n- The derivative of $\\arctan x$ is similar to the others, except that there is no square root and there are no negative signs." :tutorial/id #uuid "623e9f24-09b2-5351-803e-372a19ae8bbd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 450 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4015d8bc-2877-52dd-8272-a39219420484" :step/math-academy-id 843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064365 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064009 :question/id #uuid "75114520-8891-503c-81b7-be4c86f5d054" :question/math-academy-id "e-202"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "991